pra: 0204 - instrument the pset0 ticket-lock recursive panic (8-core bring-up)

The cpus=8 trial on PRA dies at lock_ticket.c __lck_ticket_owned_panic
(PRA-LX1-noted.md:769-795, reproduced from the Exynos donor): panic
"Ticket lock 0xfffffff007b70880 is unexpectedly owned by thread 0x...". The
address is pset0 + 0x100 = pset0.sched_lock (PRA-LX1-noted.md:780) and the
panic fires when the SECOND physical cluster (MPIDR 0x100..0x103) registers
through processor_up -> pset_lock while startAll's processor_start thread
already holds it (work/hw-8core-design.md section 4b). The stock panic names
only the RE-acquiring thread; the OWNER thread id is the missing half, so the
failure site is guessed instead of known. This patch prints both ctids (the
owner word lck_ticket_owner and self) to the console (the panic is early,
before the +30 s pralog ring - console with wdt=-1 is the primary capture,
hw-8core-design.md section 5) and extends the panic string with both.

Provenance: this panic is scheduler-internal (xnu osfmk), untouched by every
PRA patch so far - the hunk base is the post-0203 full-stack file (the
function has been stable since xnu-8796). kprintf and panic are already
available at this point (panic with context records __builtin_return_address).

Ring/console lines that name the bug:
    PRA LOCK: ticket 0xfffffff007b70880 owned by ctid 0x..., re-acquired by
        ctid 0x...
    and the panic string with both ctids.

FALSIFIER (the discriminator): with 0204, the cpus=8 boot either shows the
owner ctid = the startAll thread (ktrace/thread name in a later stage or the
console) = the race is startAll's processor_start holding pset0 while the
secondary's processor_up re-locks it - serializing/deferring cluster-1 start
is the fix; or owner ctid = some OTHER thread (clutch rebalance, scheduler
tick) = the recursive acquire is introduced elsewhere and the next
instrumentation moves thereandarounds the pset0 lock. Either outcome names
the fix for 0205; a boot that reaches 8/8 "SGI1 verified" falsifies the
reproducer itself (the trial is command-line gated: cpus=8, PRA_ALLOW_ANY_
CMDLINE=1).

NOTE: this patch is DIAGNOSTIC ONLY and does not change scheduler behaviour.
The cpus=8 flash that exercises it requires explicit user approval (the
cluster-1 scheduler panic is the expected outcome; wdt=-1 keeps it on
screen for capture).

BASE: the post-0203 full-stack osfmk/kern/lock_ticket.c. Only this hunk;
fuzz=0 applies clean and the full-stack git apply still reports zero skips.
--- a/osfmk/kern/lock_ticket.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/lock_ticket.c
@@ -140,7 +140,18 @@
 {
 	thread_t self = current_thread();
 
-	panic("Ticket lock %p is unexpectedly owned by thread %p", lck, self);
+	/*
+	 * 0204 (8-core): the stock panic names only the re-acquiring thread, not
+	 * who holds the lock. On PRA the cpus=8 trial dies here on pset0.sched_lock
+	 * (PRA-LX1-noted.md:780) while registering cluster 1 - the owner ctid is
+	 * the missing half. ctid is a system-wide compact thread id (thread.h:778),
+	 * unique per thread; the kprintf lands in the pralog ring only after +30 s,
+	 * so the console (wdt=-1) is the primary capture for this early panic.
+	 */
+	kprintf("PRA LOCK: ticket %p owned by ctid 0x%x, re-acquired by ctid 0x%x\n",
+	    lck, (unsigned)tlock->lck_ticket_owner, (unsigned)self->ctid);
+	panic("Ticket lock %p is unexpectedly owned by thread %p (ctid 0x%x, owner 0x%x)",
+	    lck, self, (unsigned)self->ctid, (unsigned)tlock->lck_ticket_owner);
 }
 
 __abortlike
